    {
      "id": "development_impact",
      "name": "Development Impact",
      "weight": null,
      "note": "Taken from clause 5 of the People's Bank form, which scores nine before/after development outcomes, and clause 3.4.1, which disaggregates employment by gender. Mainstream credit scoring optimises repayment probability alone and discards these; a state bank with a development mandate cannot. NOTE ON NOVELTY: scoring social impact alongside credit risk is NOT new - see Gutierrez-Nieto, Serrano-Cinca & Camon-Cala (2016, J. Business Ethics). What differs here is that the two objectives are reported SEPARATELY and never collapsed into one number, so a weak-development/strong-credit case stays visible as such. That separation was originally justified on Arvanitis, Stampini & Vencatachellum (2015) as an independence result. THAT READING WAS WRONG: they report a positive but non-significant relationship (slope 0.048, p = 0.49) across 109 operations, which cannot distinguish independence from a moderate association. Measured directly on 652,284 facilities the two objectives correlate at r = +0.40. The separation stands on a different basis - the objectives place the same facility in DIFFERENT risk bands 88.2% of the time, so aggregation destroys information about individual cases whether or not the two correlate on average.",
